246:\paragraph{Honest identity churn (exploratory follow-up).} In the registered markets every honest provider keeps one identity for the whole episode, so the newest identities are mostly the attacker's. After the report we let each of the nine honest opponents retire its identity and re-enter under a fresh one with probability $p$ in each round, $p = \const{0.01}$ or $p = \const{0.05}$ (mean identity lifetimes of \const{100} and \const{20} rounds; \site{ch_reps1} and \site{ch_reps5} honest replacements per episode), and reran the registered D2+H3 search, evaluation, and doubling check for B3, R3, and C$^*$ on the six fresh markets at $\rho=\const{0.2}$, each against the rule's frozen truthful reference played under the same churn. The rates are illustrative sensitivity settings, not calibrated to any market. The plan and four expectations were written before any churn search, after a timing check on one market (one rule, the old attack, no reference) had been seen. The churn model keeps capacity and quality fixed, but it also resets an honest provider's history and lets it leave a false ban, so it does not isolate age information. This is an exploratory follow-up; it changes no registered decision, and its intervals hold the six markets and the selected policies fixed. At $p = \const{0.05}$, the pooled gain against R3 rises from \site{ch_r3_0} to \site{ch_r3_5} (paired seed-bootstrap interval [\site{ch_r3_5_lo}, \site{ch_r3_5_hi}]), while the gains against C$^*$ and B3 change much less (from \site{ch_c_0} to \site{ch_c_5}, and from \site{ch_b_0} to \site{ch_b_5}). The C$^*$ minus R3 difference falls from \site{ch_d_0} to \site{ch_d_5} ([\site{ch_d_5_lo}, \site{ch_d_5_hi}]; negative on \site{ch_d_5_neg} of the six markets), and the B3 minus R3 gap becomes \site{ch_q_5} ([\site{ch_q_5_lo}, \site{ch_q_5_hi}]; negative on \site{ch_q_5_neg} markets): under this churn, the attack earns more against R3 than against the rule without audit priority. The rise comes from the attack's profit, not from a weaker reference: against R3, the attack's mean profit rises by \site{ch_pa_5} and the reference's by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]). At $p = \const{0.01}$, the C$^*$ minus R3 difference is \site{ch_d_1} ([\site{ch_d_1_lo}, \site{ch_d_1_hi}]; doubled searches \site{ch_d_1_d}), and the B3 minus R3 gap stays at \site{ch_q_1}. The diagnostics show how. At $p = \const{0.05}$, over the rounds in which the attacker holds assigned jobs, its identity is the newest among those holding assigned jobs, the order in which R3 ranks identities, in \site{ch_new_r3} of the rounds under R3 (\site{ch_new_c} under C$^*$, \site{ch_new_b} under B3). R3 audits \site{ch_sh_r3} of the jobs that honest replacement identities serve in their first ten rounds, against \site{ch_sh_c} under C$^*$ and \site{ch_sh_b} under B3, pooled over the markets. R3's newcomer priority thus moves audits to honest newcomers while the attacker's identity hides among them. The expectations set in advance, scored on point estimates under both the frozen and the doubled searches: pooled $g_{\const{0.05}}(\mathrm{R3})$ above its no-churn value, ``\site{ch_e1}''; pooled C$^*$ minus R3 at $p = \const{0.05}$ below its no-churn value, ``\site{ch_e2}''; B3 minus R3 positive on all six markets at $p = \const{0.01}$, ``\site{ch_e3}''; the audited share of honest replacements in their first ten rounds higher under R3 than under C$^*$ and B3 at $p = \const{0.05}$, ``\site{ch_e4}''. \site{ch_inc} of the \site{ch_inc_n} doubling checks are flagged incomplete; at $p = \const{0.05}$ the doubled searches give the same sign for every pooled gain and contrast, while at $p = \const{0.01}$ the C$^*$ minus R3 difference changes sign under doubling (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn}). Within this model, at the higher rate, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ and B3 does not survive honest churn.
