ity-weighted mean of its components' gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing evaluation records. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_e_cs} for C$^*$ at non-negative estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3's mixture (\site{r_ph_e_r3_d}) meets the registered training constraint, and C$^*$'s does so at the smaller weight \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, with efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs} against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). Weights and companions were chosen on the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided \const{95} percent bound, so these rows show that the prescribed W1 search did not consider episode-level mixtures that reach lower empirical efficiency; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to 